          "path": "_m/r/b/s/m/space-place-at-carter-observatory-wp/thomas-cooke-telescope.jpg",
          "filename": "Thomas Cooke Telescope.jpg",
          "caption": "The Thomas Cooke telescope is the main telescope in regular use at Space Place. It is a 9.75-inch Cooke refractor, manufactured in 1867 in York, England, and has been housed at the Observatory since it opened in 1941. Visible in the lower right is the Grubb &amp; Parsons clock drive, which was added to the telescope in 1879 and serves as the telescope's mechanical tracking system. The 5-inch W. Watson &amp; Sons guide scope (attached directly to the left of the main scope) was added in either 1896 or 1905.",
          "alt": "The Thomas Cooke telescope is the main telescope in regular use at Space Place. It is a 9.75-inch Cooke refractor, manufactured in 1867 in York, England, and has been housed at the Observatory since it opened in 1941. Visible in the lower right is the Grubb &amp; Parsons clock drive, which was added to the telescope in 1879 and serves as the telescope's mechanical tracking system. The 5-inch W. Watson &amp; Sons guide scope (attached directly to the left of the main scope) was added in either 1896 or 1905.",
          "width": 2988,
          "height": 5312,
          "mimeType": "image/jpeg",
          "license": "CC BY-SA 4.0",
          "artist": "SPCarterObservatory",
          "sourceUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thomas_Cooke_Telescope.jpg",
